WEBVTT

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:26.710
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life , distributed by Public Radio International. I'm Ira Glass. Long ago, when our radio show was first on the air, we tried an experiment, two experiments, actually. We did two of our shows onstage in Chicago within six months of each other. In the first, at this small club called the Lunar Cabaret, we had a kind of open mic, where people came onstage and they read letters, letters that they'd received, letters that they'd written, letters that they'd found.
<v Ira Glass>来自芝加哥WBEZ电台，这是《美国生活》节目，由国际公共广播电台（PRI）发行。我是艾拉·格拉斯。很久以前，当我们的广播节目刚开播时，我们做了一个实验，其实是两个实验。我们在六个月内先后在芝加哥举办了两场现场节目。第一场在一家名为“月球歌舞厅”的小俱乐部，我们设置了一种类似开放麦的环节，人们上台朗读信件，读他们收到的信、写的信，或是捡到的信。

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:43.480
<v Ira Glass>The second show, at the Museum of Contemporary Art, was all about this new phenomenon called the internet. This was 1997, and most people were still just discovering the internet. And we invited our listeners to come on and tell us stories about things that were happening on the web that never would have happened anywhere else.
<v Ira Glass>第二场节目是在当代艺术博物馆举办的，主题是当时一个叫“互联网”的新事物。那是1997年，大多数人还处于刚刚发现互联网的阶段。我们邀请听众上台讲述那些发生在网络上，而且绝不可能在其他地方发生的奇闻轶事。

00:00:43.480 --> 00:01:19.760
<v Ira Glass>Today on our radio show, we're going to go back to those two shows. They're like little time capsules, those shows. The internet stuff, which is going to be the second half of today's program, it just seems so quaint that we were asking ourselves, how big is this internet thing going to be? Is it really going to change things? And the letters that people read onstage that we're going to play in the first half of  the show, even that feels like it's from a completely other time. Like, today, who writes letters? When do you write a letter except for a thank you note or a letter to get a job? Even soldiers in Iraq, when they write home to their families, they use email.
<v Ira Glass>今天我们的广播节目将重温那两场演出。那些节目就像小小的时光胶囊。关于互联网的内容，也就是今天节目的下半部分，我们当时问自己“互联网到底会有多大？”“它真的会改变一切吗？”这种想法现在看来真是古朴得可爱。而在上半部分中，人们在台上朗读的那些信件，读起来也仿佛来自另一个时代。比如，现在还有谁会写信呢？除了感谢卡或是求职信，你什么时候会写信？即使是驻伊拉克的士兵，给家里写信时用的也是电子邮件。

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:37.140
<v Ira Glass>My co-host for both of these old shows was a Chicago playwright named David Hauptschein. David had been hosting and organizing events like this around Chicago where he would have people come onstage and read letters. So let's go back in time to 1996 and 1997.
<v Ira Glass>那两场老节目的联合主持人是一位名叫大卫·豪普沙因的芝加哥剧作家。大卫当时就在芝加哥各地组织这类活动，请人们上台读信。那么，让我们回到1996年和1997年吧。

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:50.590
<v David Hauptschein>All right, you see this egg timer here? This egg timer will get you off the stage after five minutes. When you hear [BELL RING] that, you must stop. If you're really nervous, I have this little elephant for people to hold, in case they're really--
<v David Hauptschein>好了，看到这个计时器了吗？这个计时器能让你在五分钟后离开舞台。当你听到（铃声）那个声音时，你必须停下来。如果你真的很紧张，我这儿有个小象玩偶可以让你拿着，以防你……

00:01:50.590 --> 00:01:50.930
<v Ira Glass>He's holding up a--
<v Ira Glass>他举起了一个……

00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:53.030
<v David Hauptschein>Oh, it's a rhino. Sorry, sorry, sorry.
<v David Hauptschein>哦，那是只犀牛。抱歉，抱歉，抱歉。

00:01:53.030 --> 00:01:56.460
<v Ira Glass>You're holding up about a six-inch long, gray rhinoceros, fluffy rhinoceros.
<v Ira Glass>你手里拿的是一只大约六英寸长、灰色的毛绒犀牛。

00:01:56.460 --> 00:02:01.430
<v David Hauptschein>So I will leave this up here on the podium. So if anyone gets really tense, just hang on to this.
<v David Hauptschein>那我就把它留在讲台上。所以，如果谁觉得特别紧张，就抓着它。

00:02:01.430 --> 00:02:09.110
<v Ira Glass>I hold in my hand a bowl, a silver bowl full of pieces of paper on which members of our audience here have written their names.
<v Ira Glass>我手里拿着一个碗，一个银色的碗，里面装满了观众写下他们名字的纸条。

00:02:09.110 --> 00:02:11.970
<v David Hauptschein>Please pick the first name, Ira.
<v David Hauptschein>请抽第一个名字，艾拉。

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:14.320
<v Ira Glass>John Beaderman, come on up.
<v Ira Glass>约翰·比德曼，上来吧。

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:23.060
<v Ira Glass>[APPLAUSE]
<v Ira Glass>（掌声）

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:53.780
<v John Beaderman>OK, I'll read you a couple things from Carolyn. Hiya, hun. What are you up to? I'm listening to Motley Crue, Shout at the Devil. That's a pretty kick-ass tape. It's Joe's, but it's still a kick-ass tape, and Vince Neil is such a fox. I'd love to go backstage at a Crue concert. I'd be in total heaven, because I know what I'd do, and I'll bet you do too. Like my green marker? I think it's cool. Explain this to me. Something fishy's going on. What? I don't know. But you're acting fishy, so don't lie, and it's nothing I did.
<v John Beaderman>好的，我来给你读两段卡罗琳写的东西。嘿，亲爱的。你在干嘛？我正在听克鲁小丑乐队的《向恶魔呐喊》。那磁带真是酷毙了。那是乔的，但依然很棒，文斯·尼尔真是个万人迷。我真想去克鲁小丑演唱会的后台。我会完全陷入天堂，因为我知道我会做什么，我敢打赌你也知道。喜欢我的绿色记号笔吗？我觉得很酷。给我解释一下这个。有什么猫腻在发生。什么？我不知道。但你的行为很可疑，所以别撒谎，这不是我做的。

00:02:53.780 --> 00:03:14.660
<v John Beaderman>I don't act any different than before we did it. Are you trying to say that I'm acting different, or I'm slightly more insane than unusual? Is that what you mean? I'm acting differently? If that's what you mean, how? I just heard Judas Priest [? talking to ?] Dio. That's pretty kick-ass. Why did Chris ask for me today? And why did you guys hang up on me? That's pretty ignorant, not to mention immature and rude.
<v John Beaderman>我的行为和我们做那事之前没什么不同。你是想说我的行为变了，还是说我比平时更疯狂了一点？你是这个意思吗？我的行为变了吗？如果是这样，怎么变的？我刚听到犹大圣徒乐队在和迪奥聊天。那真是太酷了。为什么克里斯今天问起我？你们为什么要挂我电话？这真是无知，更别提既幼稚又不礼貌了。

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:37.400
<v John Beaderman>I'm listening to Motley Crue, "Bastard" again, so they are so cool. I haven't decided if I like Crue or Maiden better yet, but they're both totally kick-ass. God, is this note [BLEEP] dumb. It's 7:30 and I want it to be 8:30 because then I can listen to RPM, Real Precious Metal 103.1. 831-1031. Kick ass! "Helter skelter, you may be a lover but you ain't no dancer, helter skelter."
<v John Beaderman>我正在听克鲁小丑乐队的《混蛋》，它们真是太酷了。我还没决定是更喜欢克鲁小丑还是铁娘子乐队，但它们都酷毙了。天哪，这张字条真是（消音）蠢透了。现在是七点半，我希望是八点半，那样我就能听RPM，真正的金属之声103.1。831-1031。酷毙了！“混乱，你可能是个恋人，但你不是个舞者，混乱。”

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:45.990
<v John Beaderman>[APPLAUSE]
<v John Beaderman>（掌声）

00:03:45.990 --> 00:03:47.030
<v David Hauptschein>OK, Ira.
<v David Hauptschein>好的，艾拉。

00:03:47.030 --> 00:03:51.580
<v Ira Glass>Next reader, Neal Pollack.
<v Ira Glass>下一位朗读者，尼尔·波拉克。

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:55.580
<v Neal Pollack>Hi. I wrote-- can I hold this?
<v Neal Pollack>嗨。我写了……我可以拿着这个吗？

00:03:55.580 --> 00:03:56.920
<v David Hauptschein>Please do.
<v David Hauptschein>请便。

00:03:56.920 --> 00:03:59.520
<v Ira Glass>Neal is holding the rhinoceros.
<v Ira Glass>尼尔拿着那只犀牛。

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:18.360
<v Neal Pollack>I wrote these letters soon after I graduated from college. And I like to turn back to them every now and then to remind myself what an idiot I was. I believed at the time that I could get any job I wanted anywhere at any point. And these letters, I think, are indicative.
<v Neal Pollack>我写这些信是在我大学毕业后不久。我喜欢不时地回头读读它们，好提醒自己当初有多蠢。我当时坚信我随时随地都能得到任何我想要的工作。我想，这些信就是证据。

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:44.870
<v Neal Pollack>Here's one I wrote to Tina Brown, who was then the editor of Vanity Fair magazine. Dear Ms. Brown, I'll cut to the quick. [LAUGHTER] I would like to work for The New Yorker , and I imagine, with the big changeover, you may need lots of editorial help. So I'm throwing my hat into the ring and asking you to consider hiring me as an editorial assistant for your new project.
<v Neal Pollack>这是我写给蒂娜·布朗的一封，她当时是《名利场》杂志的主编。亲爱的布朗女士，我长话短说。（笑声）我想为《纽约客》工作，我想随着这次大的变动，你们可能需要很多编辑方面的帮助。所以我毛遂自荐，希望你能考虑雇我担任你们新项目的编辑助理。

00:04:44.870 --> 00:05:11.460
<v Neal Pollack>I've been precociously reading The New Yorker for some time now. Most of the writers I respect most, living and dead, have written for your magazine at some time. I'm willing to cut my journalistic and literary teeth elsewhere if I can't get a job at The New Yorker , but I am determined not to end up anywhere else. The resume I've enclosed doesn't indicate this, but I've been working as a freelance writer since graduating two months ago. [LAUGHTER]
<v Neal Pollack>我已经早慧地阅读《纽约客》很久了。我尊敬的多数作家，无论生前死后，都曾为你们杂志写过文章。如果我不能在《纽约客》得到一份工作，我愿意在别处磨练我的新闻和文学功底，但我决心不去其他任何地方。我附上的简历没写出来，但我从两个月前毕业起就一直在做自由撰稿人。（笑声）

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:29.820
<v Neal Pollack>I've been doing pretty well, but I'm still waiting for the big score. Maybe you could help. I strongly encourage you to take a chance on me, but if I don't suit your needs at this time, thank you for reviewing my application. I look forward to hearing from you. [APPLAUSE]
<v Neal Pollack>我干得还不错，但还在等待一个大机会。也许你能帮上忙。我强烈建议你给我一个机会，但如果此时不符合你的需求，谢谢你审阅我的申请。期待你的回复。（掌声）

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:53.400
<v Neal Pollack>About six months later, I hadn't yet heard from Tina Brown. So I turned my gaze elsewhere. Here's one I wrote to Strobe Talbott, who at the time was an editor-at-large at Time Magazine , and now, as you may or may not know, is a Deputy to the Secretary of State in the Clinton administration. So here's what I wrote to Strobe.
<v Neal Pollack>大约六个月后，我还没收到蒂娜·布朗的回信。所以我把目光转向了别处。这是我写给斯特罗布·塔尔博特的，他当时是《时代》杂志的特约编辑，现在，不管你知不知道，他是克林顿政府的副国务卿。这就是我写给斯特罗布的内容。

00:05:53.400 --> 00:06:29.100
<v Neal Pollack>Dear Mr. Talbott, I'm a damn good editor. I'm young and gutsy as a marine in combat. My terse, tough prose style would make Raymond Chandler weep. Somewhere, somehow, the grapevine told me that you're starting a magazine called Globe Review . Said vine also let me know that the staff will be small, which means you're not hiring too many people. [LAUGHTER]
<v Neal Pollack>亲爱的塔尔博特先生，我是一名出色的编辑。我年轻，像作战的海军陆战队员一样有胆识。我简练、强硬的文风会让雷蒙德·钱德勒落泪。不知何故，小道消息告诉我，你正在创办一本叫《环球评论》的杂志。该消息还让我知道，员工编制会很小，这意味着你不会雇佣太多人。（笑声）

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:57.280
<v Neal Pollack>Being low man on the totem pole is fine with me. Your magazine sounds original, smart, and progressive, sophisticated but not shallow. Of course, I'm just guessing. Can you go global without me? I think so. Should you? I think not. Or at least hope not. I'll do good work as an assistant editor, copy editor, or researcher.
<v Neal Pollack>在等级制度的最底层对我来说没问题。你的杂志听起来原创、聪明、进步、成熟但不浅薄。当然，我只是在猜测。没有我，你能走向全球吗？我想可以。你应该这样做吗？我认为不应该。或者至少希望不要。无论作为助理编辑、校对还是研究员，我都能胜任。

00:06:57.280 --> 00:07:22.950
<v Neal Pollack>I'll be in New York City from November 11 through 17. If you'd like to interview me, you can contact me at the Chicago address on my resume until then. Thank you for reviewing my application, and I look forward to seeing the magazine, whether I'm part of it or not. [APPLAUSE]
<v Neal Pollack>我将于11月11日到17日在纽约市。如果你想面试我，在此之前你可以通过我简历上的芝加哥地址联系我。谢谢你审阅我的申请，无论我是否参与其中，我都期待看到这本杂志。（掌声）

00:07:22.950 --> 00:07:27.360
<v Ira Glass>Next reader, Jenny Magnus.
<v Ira Glass>下一位朗读者，珍妮·马格努斯。

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:49.430
<v Jenny Magnus>All right, this is a letter that I received. It's addressed to me. So my name is Jenny Magnus. And on the envelope it says, "Lessons for Advanced Beginners." And then on the back, it says, "burn after reading."
<v Jenny Magnus>好的，这是一封我收到的信。是写给我的。我叫珍妮·马格努斯。信封上写着“高级初学者课程”。背面写着“阅后即焚”。

00:07:49.430 --> 00:08:22.260
<v Jenny Magnus>Dear Ms. Magnus, I almost fell off my chair when, during one of your pieces, you implied that you are, quote, "a really good artist." After sitting there listening to you chant "mama, mama, mama, mama, mama," which you got from Janov's book Primal Scream-- I shop through paperbacks at thrift stores, too-- then hearing you chant "give it to me, give it to me, give it to me, give it to me, give it to me," I sat there wondering just how conventional some pretentious chick dressed in black can be.
<v Jenny Magnus>亲爱的马格努斯女士，当你在某件作品中暗示自己是“一个非常好的艺术家”时，我差点从椅子上摔下来。看着你坐在那里一遍遍吟唱着从贾诺夫的《原始呐喊》书里学来的“妈妈，妈妈，妈妈，妈妈，妈妈”——我在旧货店买平装书也是这样——然后听你吟唱“给我，给我，给我，给我，给我”，我坐在那里心想，一个穿着黑衣、装腔作势的女孩到底能有多世俗。

00:08:22.260 --> 00:08:55.150
<v Jenny Magnus>I, by the way, use "chick" lightly, since you are not very feminine. In fact, you are nothing but a contrived, self-deceiving, selfish, not overly bright, mediocre joke. You might be able to fool the poseurs who hang around Urbis Orbis flattering your brother pretending to know, but not me, babe. Not me, babe. I use "babe" lightly. You are not a babe. You have-- [LAUGHTER] You have no sensuality, no charm, no looks, et cetera.
<v Jenny Magnus>顺便说一句，我用“女孩”这个词很轻，因为你一点也不女性化。事实上，你只是一个矫揉造作、自欺欺人、自私、不太聪明、平庸的笑话。你也许能骗过那些在乌比斯·奥比斯附近游荡、恭维你兄弟、假装懂行的装逼犯，但骗不了我，宝贝。骗不了我，宝贝。我用“宝贝”这个词很轻。你不是个宝贝。你——（笑声）你没有性感，没有魅力，没有长相，等等。

00:08:55.150 --> 00:09:14.870
<v Jenny Magnus>Without affectation, without deliberately taking steps to appear avant, where would you be? Nothing comes naturally to you, does it? You really have to steal and grub and labor. In fact, you are perusing this letter for material right now, I bet. [LAUGHTER]
<v Jenny Magnus>如果没有这些做作，如果没有刻意地去装前卫，你会在哪里？没有什么东西是自然而然产生的，对吧？你真的必须去窃取、去贪婪、去劳作。事实上，我敢打赌你现在就在翻这封信找素材。（笑声）

00:09:14.870 --> 00:09:35.440
<v Jenny Magnus>I realized how repressed and affected you are during "Loser's Alias." And thank God for the fire which started, or you'd probably still be on the floor reciting your brother's tripe. You are just too, too uptight, baby. You think you can act uninhibited, but why do you want to? Is it that important to you that you seem to yourself and others as if you are special?
<v Jenny Magnus>在《失败者的化名》那场演出中，我意识到你是多么压抑和做作。感谢上帝发生了那场火灾，否则你可能还在地板上背诵你兄弟的废话。你太紧绷了，宝贝。你以为你可以表现得无拘无束，但你为什么要这么做呢？让你自己和别人觉得你很特别就那么重要吗？

00:09:35.440 --> 00:10:07.440
<v Jenny Magnus>You are quite interesting, the only person with absolutely no substance I've ever met. How did you get so [BLEEP]? Give up art. You're no good at it, anyway. And save your [BLEEP] ass. Sincerely, Mason 32 Degrees. PS, well, I've done my good deed for tonight. [APPLAUSE]
<v Jenny Magnus>你真是有趣，是我见过的唯一一个完全没有内涵的人。你是怎么变得这么（消音）的？放弃艺术吧。反正你不擅长。并且省省你那（消音）屁股吧。真诚地，梅森32度。附言，好吧，我今晚做了件好事。（掌声）

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:21.780
<v Ira Glass>Our next reader, pulled from our bowl of readers, Adam Davidson. [APPLAUSE]
<v Ira Glass>下一位朗读者，从我们的碗里抽出来的，亚当·戴维森。（掌声）

00:10:21.780 --> 00:10:45.010
<v Adam Davidson>OK. This is age 16. Dear Adam, I can lose my guard best when I write. No wonder I scared off S with my letter. Speaking of which, I didn't hear from him. You see, how can I ever think of hurting you like that? Let's say something starts between us. What about S? I may not like him, but maybe I do.
<v Adam Davidson>好的。这是16岁时写的。亲爱的亚当，当我写作时，我最容易放下戒备。难怪我用那封信吓跑了S。说到这个，我没收到他的回音。你看，我怎么会想那样伤害你呢？假设我们之间开始了一些什么。那S怎么办？我可能不喜欢他，但也可能喜欢。

00:10:45.010 --> 00:11:01.990
<v Adam Davidson>Also, what if I'm just on the rebound from Dave? Not that I didn't like you before-- maybe I'm just free to say it now-- but I might be exaggerating it because I'm feeling a loss. Maybe we're confusing a really good friendship for love, et cetera. You know the deal.
<v Adam Davidson>另外，如果我只是在大卫之后寻求安慰呢？并不是说我以前不喜欢你——也许我只是现在才有勇气说出来——但我可能是在夸大它，因为我感到一种失落。也许我们把非常好的友谊误认为是爱情，等等。你懂的。

00:11:01.990 --> 00:11:28.520
<v Adam Davidson>Also, let's say we do have sex. It'll be a bigger deal for you. Not that I won't take it seriously. I have seriously thought about it. But it's a big responsibility to put on me, your first time, et cetera. Now who's babbling? Anyway, [LAUGHTER] I think you're a wonderful person and friend, and the last thing I want to do is screw that up.
<v Adam Davidson>而且，假设我们真的发生了性关系。这对你来说会是件大事。并不是说我不严肃对待。我已经认真考虑过了。但这对我来说责任太重，这是你的第一次，等等。现在是谁在胡言乱语？不管怎样，（笑声）我觉得你是一个很棒的人和朋友，我最不想做的就是搞砸这一切。

00:11:28.520 --> 00:11:49.160
<v Adam Davidson>Maybe we should just see, trial-run, if making out agrees with us. You never know, we might decide it doesn't feel right and keep things status quo. But we might end up getting it on every day. Excuse the vulgar language. As that guy in English class would say, "there is no pretty way to look at sex."
<v Adam Davidson>也许我们应该先试试，试验一下，看看亲热是否适合我们。谁知道呢，也许我们可能会觉得感觉不对，然后保持现状。但也可能我们最终会每天都腻在一起。请原谅粗俗的语言。正如英语课上那个家伙说的，“看待性没有优雅的方式。”

00:11:49.160 --> 00:11:58.260
<v Adam Davidson>Anyway, I love you, but I'm not sure what kind of love. Love, K. Geez, I don't even know what attracts me to you. You're so [BLEEP] perfect I guess.
