00:16:21.700 --> 00:17:37.770
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, a guy offers a girl a late night tour of Microsoft, and this actually makes him seem hot. That, and other hard-to-believe tales from the early days of the internet, from Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>即将播出，一个家伙给女孩提供了一次深夜参观微软的机会，这竟然让他看起来很迷人。这，以及其他关于互联网早期阶段难以置信的故事，来自芝加哥公共广播电台和国际公共广播电台，我们的节目继续。

00:17:37.770 --> 00:18:03.420
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life . I'm Ira Glass. If you're just tuning in, we're revisiting two live shows that we did in 1996 and '97, onstage in Chicago. In one show, listeners brought letters and read them onstage. And now we turn to the other show from back then. Back then, the internet was still new to most of us. And so we decided we were going to do a show where we would try to figure out what was happening on the internet, especially if things were happening on the internet that would never happen anyplace else.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。如果你刚调过来，我们正在重温我们在1996年和1997年在芝加哥舞台上做的两场现场节目。在一场节目中，听众带信来并在台上朗读。现在我们转向那时的另一场节目。那时，互联网对我们大多数人来说还是很新的事物。所以我们决定做一个节目，试图弄清楚互联网上正在发生什么，特别是如果网上发生了一些在其他任何地方都不会发生的事情。

00:18:03.420 --> 00:18:21.190
<v Ira Glass>And to answer that question, we advertised in cities around the country, inviting people to send us their stories about life on the internet. Some of these people I interviewed, and then others just showed up at the theater, at the Museum of Contemporary Art in Chicago, the night we did the show. And they came up to the mic, and they read from emails or web pages.
<v Ira Glass>为了回答这个问题，我们在全国各地的城市做了广告，邀请人们把他们关于互联网生活的故事发给我们。有些人我采访过，另一些人则是在我们演出当晚直接出现在芝加哥当代艺术博物馆的剧院里。他们走上麦克风，朗读电子邮件或网页上的内容。

00:18:21.190 --> 00:18:44.800
<v Ira Glass>And I have to say, listening to this show, it's almost adorably quaint, the entire mission of this show. It's almost embarrassing, actually, to hear me and other people trying to think through this question, like, what is this internet? What is new about the internet? You'll hear there is a point where I'm talking to a woman who's looking through the websites of complete strangers. And it's like, can you believe it? She's looking through the websites of complete strangers.
<v Ira Glass>我不得不说，听这场节目，整个节目的使命几乎可爱得古朴。事实上，听我和其他人试图思考这个问题，比如“什么是互联网？”“互联网有什么新奇的？”甚至让人感到尴尬。你会听到我当时和一位正在浏览完全陌生人网站的女士交谈。就像是，“你能相信吗？她在浏览完全陌生人的网站。”

00:18:44.800 --> 00:19:11.890
<v Ira Glass>And then she has this experience, which now is incredibly common, where she meets in person somebody who she first met on the internet. And we're all talking about it like, can you believe that such a thing can happen in this world that we live in? You'll hear. It's really interesting. It's like a historic document. So without further ado, let us turn on the time machine and go back to 1997. [APPLAUSE]
<v Ira Glass>然后她有了这种现在非常普遍的经历，就是见到了她在网上认识的人。我们都在讨论它，好像，“你能相信在我们生活的这个世界里竟然会发生这样的事吗？”你会听到的。这真的很有趣。它就像一份历史文献。所以不再多说，让我们打开时光机回到1997年吧。（掌声）

00:19:11.890 --> 00:19:20.740
<v Ira Glass>So Mary is 19 years old, an English major at the University of Washington. And one day, she's cruising the net, visiting the home pages of complete strangers.
<v Ira Glass>玛丽今年19岁，是华盛顿大学的英语专业学生。有一天，她在网上冲浪，访问完全陌生人的主页。

00:19:20.740 --> 00:19:47.170
<v Mary>And I came across this site that I thought was amazing. It was the most cutting-edge, thought-provoking site I had ever seen. And so I was examining it, checking it out, and I realized that this guy was my age, and he worked at Microsoft. And that just blew me away. Because I'm a student, I can barely afford coffee in the morning. And here, this guy worked at Microsoft.
<v Mary>我偶然发现了一个网站，我觉得太棒了。这是我见过最尖端、最发人深省的网站。所以我仔细检查，查看它，我意识到这个家伙和我同岁，而且他在微软工作。这让我大吃一惊。因为我是学生，早上连咖啡都喝不起。而在这里，这家伙在微软工作。

00:19:47.170 --> 00:20:10.510
<v Mary>I ended up emailing him just to tell him how much I liked his site and how amazing I thought it was. And he wrote back. And that went on for about four or five days. We just kept writing back and forth. Really personal stuff, too, not just computer geek stuff.
<v Mary>我最终给他发了电子邮件，只是告诉他我有多喜欢他的网站，以及我觉得它有多棒。他回信了。这种情况持续了四五天。我们不断地写回信。非常私人的东西，不仅仅是电脑极客的东西。

00:20:10.510 --> 00:20:29.970
<v Ira Glass>What is the brave new world of the internet? You can meet a stranger and get to know them intimately, much faster on the net than you can anywhere else. But for a week, Mary and this guy wrote each other several times a day, long, personal emails. Then they agreed to meet in person at a coffee shop on Valentine's Day.
<v Ira Glass>互联网的美丽新世界是什么？你可以结识一个陌生人，在网上比在任何其他地方都更快地亲密接触。但有一周的时间，玛丽和这个家伙每天互发几封很长的私人电子邮件。然后他们约定在情人节那天在一家咖啡馆见面。

00:20:29.970 --> 00:20:43.520
<v Mary>I was nervous before we met, because I was worried that he wouldn't find me as interesting in real life. So we met for coffee. We ended up spending the next 12 hours together.
<v Mary>见面之前我很紧张，因为我担心他在现实生活中不会觉得我很有趣。所以我们见了咖啡。最终我们在一起度过了接下来的12个小时。

00:20:43.520 --> 00:21:08.230
<v Mary>There wasn't really a turning point until a few days later. We had continued to see each other. And one night, we ended up going out to his office at Microsoft. It was about midnight and nobody was there. And we were hanging out, and one thing led to another. And all of a sudden, we were making out.
<v Mary>直到几天后才有一个转折点。我们继续见面。一天晚上，我们最终去了他在微软的办公室。大约是午夜，那里没人。我们在那闲逛，事情自然而然就发生了。突然间，我们就在亲热了。

00:21:08.230 --> 00:21:09.480
<v Ira Glass>And?
<v Ira Glass>然后呢？

00:21:09.480 --> 00:21:11.990
<v Mary>It was crazy.
<v Mary>太疯狂了。

00:21:11.990 --> 00:21:14.000
<v Ira Glass>And you were glad, though, because you were liking him.
<v Ira Glass>不过你很高兴，因为你喜欢他。

00:21:14.000 --> 00:21:26.870
<v Mary>Oh yeah, yeah. It wasn't a bad thing. But it was awkward because it was my first time. I had never really done that with anybody before.
<v Mary>哦，是的，是的。那不是坏事。但那很尴尬，因为那是我的第一次。我以前从未和任何人那样做过。

00:21:26.870 --> 00:21:30.890
<v Ira Glass>It was your very first time actually having sex with somebody?
<v Ira Glass>那是你真正和别人发生性关系的第一次？

00:21:30.890 --> 00:21:36.010
<v Mary>Right, well, no. It was my first time kissing anybody.
<v Mary>对，嗯，不是。那是我第一次亲吻任何人。

00:21:36.010 --> 00:21:37.340
<v Ira Glass>Kissing anybody at all?
<v Ira Glass>亲吻任何人？

00:21:37.340 --> 00:21:39.200
<v Mary>Yeah.
<v Mary>是的。

00:21:39.200 --> 00:21:42.050
<v Ira Glass>Because you hadn't had a high school boyfriend who you would do that with?
<v Ira Glass>因为你以前没有高中男朋友和你做过那个？

00:21:42.050 --> 00:21:48.610
<v Mary>No. No. I was a real big loner in high school. I didn't get out much.
<v Mary>没有。没有。我在高中是个很大的独行侠。我不怎么出门。

00:21:48.610 --> 00:21:51.450
<v Ira Glass>So it was your first time just making out with somebody and it was at Microsoft.
<v Ira Glass>所以这是你第一次和别人亲热，而且是在微软。

00:21:51.450 --> 00:22:12.380
<v Mary>Yeah. And so it made it all the more surreal, I think. It was just weird for me to think that, here's this guy I met a little over a week ago by chance on the internet. And here we are at his office doing this.
<v Mary>是的。所以我认为这让一切显得更加超现实。想到这里，这个家伙是我一周多前偶然在互联网上认识的，而现在我们在这里做这件事，对我来说太奇怪了。

00:22:12.380 --> 00:22:29.330
<v Mary>And then after that, I barely heard from him. He told me that he didn't want a relationship right now, that he wanted to wait. He kept using the term, he wanted to make his first million before he had a relationship with somebody.
<v Mary>然后在那之后，我几乎没再收到他的消息。他告诉我，他现在不想建立恋爱关系，他想等等。他一直用这个词，他想在和别人建立恋爱关系之前赚到他的第一个一百万。

00:22:29.330 --> 00:22:30.470
<v Ira Glass>His first million?
<v Ira Glass>他的第一个一百万？

00:22:30.470 --> 00:22:43.630
<v Mary>Yes. After the fact, after the night at his office down there at Microsoft, I noticed, boy, he really was feeding me some lines there.
<v Mary>是的。事后，在微软办公室那个晚上之后，我注意到，天哪，他真的在那儿对我撒谎。

