00:24:02.280 --> 00:24:13.830
<v Joe Fosco>This one I'm going to read now is a website of this guy who's building an aluminum ball, a ball of aluminum foil. And he went into quite a bit of detail about this ball.
<v Joe Fosco>我现在要读的这个是一个家伙的网站，他正在建造一个铝球，一个锡箔球。他对这个球的细节描述得非常详细。

00:24:13.830 --> 00:24:34.270
<v Joe Fosco>He says, "I don't remember exactly how the ball began, but it is a sphere, composed entirely of aluminum foil of various varieties. It is mostly candy wrappers with a few bits of foil and other food items mixed in. So far the best finish coat is York Peppermint Patty wrappers. Other foil is fine, but a coat of Peppermint Patty wrappers is necessary to keep it on.
<v Joe Fosco>他说，“我不记得这个球是怎么开始的，但它是一个球体，完全由各种各样的锡箔纸组成。它主要是糖果包装纸，混入了一些锡箔碎片和其他食品包装。到目前为止，最好的涂层是约克薄荷糖包装纸。其他锡箔纸也可以，但必须要有薄荷糖包装纸来保持它。”

00:24:34.270 --> 00:24:53.630
<v Joe Fosco>Gum wrappers also tend to have that quality, but they are far too difficult to peel, considering the minimal bulk they have. Gum wrappers do make a good finish coat on top of Peppermint Patty wrappers for show. Some items, such as Hershey's Miniatures, which look like they might be good candidates for foil, aren't. The ball is more impressive in person than this page can hope to depict."
<v Joe Fosco>口香糖包装纸也倾向于有这种质量，但它们太难剥了，考虑到它们只有很少的体积。口香糖包装纸确实能在薄荷糖包装纸上做出很好的饰面。有些项目，比如好时迷你巧克力，看起来可能是不错的候选人，但其实不是。球体在现实中比这个页面所能描绘的要令人印象深刻得多。”

00:24:53.630 --> 00:24:54.710
<v Ira Glass>There's a picture of the ball.
<v Ira Glass>球的照片在那里。

00:24:54.710 --> 00:25:21.270
<v Joe Fosco>Oh, there is. I don't know if you can see it. [LAUGHTER] And then he has this diary of its growth. And it starts back in January. And he says, "we've gotten an electric postage scale in the office. It reads one more digit of precision, so we now have a weight of 3.9 ounces. I have done the math to compute the density of the ball. Curiously, it is much lower than that of solid aluminum.
<v Joe Fosco>哦，确实有。我不知道你们能不能看到。（笑声）然后他有这个生长的日记。它开始于一月份。他说，“办公室里有个电子邮资秤。它能多读一位精度，所以我们现在的重量是3.9盎司。我做了数学计算来计算球的密度。奇怪的是，它比固体铝的密度低得多。”

00:25:21.270 --> 00:25:48.300
<v Joe Fosco>I have a hard time believing that the ball is less than half aluminum by mass, so I invite you to let me know if there are any errors in my computations. I expected that the density would be less than that of solid aluminum because of a small amount of air between layers of the foil, the adhesive coatings, inks, and small quantities of chocolate grease and other leftovers from the food items that the wrappers were originally used to package. I wouldn't have been too surprised by a 5% or even 10% difference, but I find the actual results obtained a little difficult to believe."
<v Joe Fosco>“我很难相信这个球的铝含量按质量计不到一半，所以我邀请你让我知道我的计算是否有任何错误。我预料到密度会低于固体铝，因为箔层之间有少量的空气，粘合剂涂层，墨水，还有少量的巧克力油脂和其他原包装食品留下的残留物。我对5%甚至10%的差异不会感到太惊讶，但我发现实际获得的结果有点难以置信。”

00:25:48.300 --> 00:26:04.810
<v Joe Fosco>The other interesting thing is that on this, he has links to other sites that are making aluminum foil balls. [LAUGHTER] And someone else who is making a rubber band ball. [APPLAUSE]
<v Joe Fosco>另一个有趣的事情是，在这个页面上，他有链接指向其他正在制作锡箔球的网站。（笑声）还有其他人正在制作橡皮筋球。（掌声）

00:26:04.810 --> 00:26:08.780
<v Ira Glass>Our next presenter, our next reader, Noel.
<v Ira Glass>下一位演讲者，下一位读者，诺埃尔。

00:26:08.780 --> 00:26:26.740
<v Noel>My name is Noel, and I'm diastematic. Anybody here know what that means? It means that I have a gap between my front two teeth. For those of you unfamiliar with this whole website gap-tooth thing, I built a website for gap-tooth people. So yeah, idiotic. You want it? You've got it.
<v Noel>我叫诺埃尔，我有牙缝。这里有人知道那是什么意思吗？这意味着我的门牙之间有缝隙。对于那些不熟悉整个网站牙缝事物的人来说，我为一个牙缝人建立了一个网站。所以是的，很白痴。你想要吗？你有它。

00:26:26.740 --> 00:26:47.130
<v Noel>But I'm in good company. 10% of America, including David Letterman, Lauren Hutton, Noam Chomsky, Supreme Court Justice Sandra Day O'Connor, Itzhak Perlman, Chaucer's Wife of Bath, Vince Lombardi, blah, blah, blah, all with the tooth cleavage. I must admit, for better or for worse, yes, I'm the one who built this website. But the emphasis is humor. In early November last year--
<v Noel>但我找到了好伴侣。10%的美国人，包括大卫·莱特曼、劳伦·赫顿、诺姆·乔姆斯基、最高法院大法官桑德拉·戴·奥康纳、伊扎克·帕尔曼、乔叟的巴斯夫人、文斯·隆巴迪，等等等等，都有牙缝。我必须承认，无论好坏，是的，我就是建立这个网站的人。但重点是幽默。去年11月初——

00:26:47.130 --> 00:26:59.000
<v Ira Glass>Wait, humor versus what? Like, attacks at people without gaps in their teeth? What would be the other thing that you would do? You would proselytize on behalf-- try to bring people over, encourage people to create gaps in their teeth?
<v Ira Glass>等等，幽默对比什么？比如，攻击那些牙缝没有缝的人？你还会做其他什么事情？你会代表——试图把人拉过来，鼓励人们创造牙缝吗？

00:26:59.000 --> 00:27:29.600
<v Noel>Absolutely. This is very, this will hit on a lot of those themes. In early November last year, I was watching a Bears-Packers game. And during the pregame ceremonies, the USPS, the post office, unveiled art for a new stamp, due this summer, featuring the late, great Vince Lombardi, who was one of the greatest gappers ever to live. He was on a stamp, riding high atop the team in the throes of victory, smiling huge. But something was missing, Coach Lombardi's trademark gap-toothed grin. He had near-perfect pearly whites and the gap was gone.
<v Noel>绝对。这会击中很多这些主题。去年11月初，我在看一场熊队和包装工队的比赛。在赛前仪式上，美国邮政局揭幕了一枚新邮票的艺术，定于今年夏天，以已故伟大的文斯·隆巴迪为特色，他是有史以来最伟大的牙缝人之一。他在邮票上，骑在胜利的高处，笑得很灿烂。但少了些什么，隆巴迪教练标志性的牙缝笑容。他有近乎完美的珍珠般的白牙，缝隙不见了。

00:27:29.600 --> 00:27:44.390
<v Noel>The next day, as a joke, I added a page to the gap-tooth website, protesting the post office's depiction of Lombardi as a cosmetically-altered hero. Who would Don King be if he were bald? Or Gorbachev without the Kool-Aid stain? Or Jimmy Durante without that nose?
<v Noel>第二天，作为开玩笑，我在牙缝网站上增加了一个页面，抗议邮局对隆巴迪作为美容改变英雄的描绘。如果唐·金秃头了，那他会是谁？或者戈尔巴乔夫没有了那个红酒渍？或者吉米·杜兰特没有了那个鼻子？

00:27:44.390 --> 00:28:04.580
<v Noel>So knowing that this was the web, the oddball media that gets no real merit for its content, I expected little to no attention. But then came Yahoo, which featured it as a weekly pick. The Washington Post had me on the phone and ran a half-page story with my before and after pics. Chicago's Channel 7 came to my apartment and ran a story on its 6:00 News, just before the Super Bowl.
<v Noel>所以知道这是网络，那种内容没有真正价值的怪异媒体，我预料到很少甚至没有注意。但后来雅虎出现了，将其作为每周精选。华盛顿邮报给我打电话，刊登了半页关于我的前后对比照的故事。芝加哥第7频道来到我的公寓，在超级碗之前的6点新闻里报道了一个故事。

00:28:04.580 --> 00:28:24.210
<v Noel>Getting out of hand? Yeah, timeout! If you're thinking this pathetic web-based protest should never have received any attention, I'm in agreement with you. What was ironic about this story is that they painted me as a freak-- imagine that-- a super fan ready to strap myself with bombs and blow up my teeth and Washington, DC if they didn't change the art.
<v Noel>失控了吗？是的，暂停！如果你认为这个可悲的网络抗议不应该得到任何注意，我和你意见一致。这个故事讽刺的是，他们把我描述成一个怪胎——想象一下——一个随时准备穿上炸弹并炸掉我的牙齿和华盛顿特区的超级粉丝，如果他们不改变艺术的话。

00:28:24.210 --> 00:28:42.680
<v Noel>And I thought, I'm just running a website for gap-toothed people. You can't take me that seriously. So I called up Bob, the artist of the postage stamp, and we talked about the stamp. We discussed angles and art representation. He was a really nice guy. I was thinking about dropping the protest already when he told me his three-year-old daughter was gap-toothed.
<v Noel>我想，我只是在经营一个为牙缝人服务的网站。你不能这么认真地对待我。所以我给邮票的艺术家鲍勃打了电话，我们讨论了邮票。我们讨论了角度和艺术表现。他真是个好人。当他告诉我他三岁的女儿有牙缝时，我就已经在考虑放弃抗议了。

00:28:42.680 --> 00:29:03.340
<v Noel>And that was it. Protest over. But what still gets me today is that the web has the power to make insignificant things seem real, that email can really serve as a powerful form of protest, which it did in this case, and that people, including myself, really do have too much time on their hands. [LAUGHTER] [APPLAUSE]
<v Noel>就是这样。抗议结束了。但今天仍然让我困惑的是，网络有能力让无关紧要的事情显得真实，电子邮件真的可以成为一种强大的抗议形式，就像这个案例一样，而且人们，包括我自己，真的有太多时间在手了。（笑声）（掌声）

00:29:03.340 --> 00:29:30.070
<v Ira Glass>Well, for our next one, I thought we'd do one that people contacted us when we advertised around the country for things going on on the net. And somebody notified us about this one web page. We were searching for people who were having experiences on the net that they would never have otherwise. And this particular page was made by a woman named Jenni Ringley, a senior at Dickinson College in Pennsylvania. And like a lot of people, she created her own little home page on the world wide web.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，对于下一个，我想做一个我们在全国各地为网上发生的事情做广告时人们联系我们的故事。有人通知我们这个网页。我们正在寻找在网上有其他地方无法发生的经历的人。而这个特定的页面是由宾夕法尼亚州迪金森学院的大四学生珍妮·林利制作的。像很多人一样，她在万维网上创建了自己的小主页。

00:29:30.070 --> 00:29:54.600
<v Jenni Ringley>There's the regular part of my page, I guess you would say, with information about me, the music I like, things like that, like everybody has on their page. The one item that has become extremely popular, though, is the JenniCam. And it's just a camera that sits in my room and takes a picture every three minutes and uploads it. So every three minutes, you can find out what fascinating thing I'm doing in my room.
<v Jenni Ringley>我想你会说我的页面有常规部分，有关于我的信息，我喜欢的音乐，像那样的东西，就像每个人页面上都有的一样。不过，变得非常流行的一项是珍妮摄像头。它只是一个坐在我房间里的摄像头，每三分钟拍一张照片并上传。所以每三分钟，你就可以发现我在房间里做什么迷人的事情。

00:29:54.600 --> 00:30:03.360
<v Ira Glass>The JenniCam is on seven days a week, 24 hours a day, and the number of people who want to see what fascinating thing is going on in a college girl's dorm room each day?
<v Ira Glass>珍妮摄像头每周7天，每天24小时开启，每天想要看一个大学女生的宿舍里正在发生什么迷人事情的人数？

00:30:03.360 --> 00:30:08.980
<v Jenni Ringley>People, I'm not sure. But as far as number of hits go, we get over 500,000 hits a day.
<v Jenni Ringley>人，我不确定。但就点击量而言，我们每天有超过50万的点击。

00:30:08.980 --> 00:30:13.360
<v Ira Glass>A half million hits every single day.
<v Ira Glass>每天50万次点击。

00:30:13.360 --> 00:30:36.980
<v Ira Glass>OK, I'm just going to stop the recording right there. This is Ira. This is me in the present. This number right here, this number, a half million hits, we had no way to confirm that at the time. And not only that, we had no idea how to interpret that number. Like, it just seemed absurdly high, the idea that each page hit didn't necessarily represent a distinct person, that thought had not totally penetrated the culture.
<v Ira Glass>好的，我就在这里停止录音。这是艾拉。这是现在的我。就在这里，这个50万次点击的数字，我们当时没有办法确认。不仅如此，我们根本不知道如何解释这个数字。比如，它看起来荒谬地高，认为每个页面点击不一定代表一个不同的人，这种想法还没有完全渗透到文化中。

00:30:36.980 --> 00:30:52.390
<v Ira Glass>And if you think about a photo every three minutes on this website, somebody staying on that site an hour might be counted as 20 separate hits. You'd only need 25,000 people to get to a half million. OK, anyway, let's get back.
<v Ira Glass>如果你考虑这个网站上每三分钟一张照片，在一个小时里呆在这个网站上的人可能会被计算为20次单独的点击。你只需要25000人就可以达到50万。好的，不管怎样，让我们回来。

00:30:52.390 --> 00:30:59.830
<v Ira Glass>A half million hits every single day. And what do people see?
<v Ira Glass>每天50万次点击。人们看到了什么？

00:30:59.830 --> 00:31:13.170
<v Jenni Ringley>Nothing, really. I write email. I sleep. I have friends over. You can watch my hedgehog when I'm not in the room. Things like that. Pretty much, just regular college life as far as I know.
<v Jenni Ringley>真的没什么。我写电子邮件。我睡觉。我有朋友过来。当我不在这房间时，你可以看我的刺猬。像这样的事情。差不多，据我所知，只是普通的大学生活。

00:31:13.170 --> 00:31:25.930
<v Ira Glass>Our associate producer tuned in to your page just to see what was going on. And for about a half an hour, she witnessed you on the phone. Every three minutes, there'd be another picture of you in a different position on the telephone.
<v Ira Glass>我们的助理制片人调到你的页面只是为了看看发生了什么。大约半小时，她目击你在电话上。每三分钟，就会有另一张你在电话上不同位置的照片。

00:31:25.930 --> 00:31:32.480
<v Jenni Ringley>I talk on the phone a lot.
<v Jenni Ringley>我经常打电话。

00:31:32.480 --> 00:31:37.180
<v Ira Glass>In a way, it's hard to think of anything more banal than seeing a college student's dorm room.
<v Ira Glass>从某种程度上讲，很难想到比看一个大学生的宿舍更平庸的事情。

00:31:37.180 --> 00:31:52.730
<v Jenni Ringley>I would have to agree. I would absolutely agree. I don't do anything that's that interesting. I don't have company very often. I don't do anything more interesting really than talk on the phone, watch TV, and sleep.
<v Jenni Ringley>我必须同意。我绝对同意。我不做任何特别有趣的事情。我很少有客人在。除了打电话、看电视和睡觉，我真的不做任何更有趣的事情。

00:31:52.730 --> 00:31:55.600
<v Ira Glass>Let me ask you to talk about the nudity.
<v Ira Glass>让我请你谈谈裸露。

00:31:55.600 --> 00:31:59.490
<v Jenni Ringley>Well, whenever I'm nude in my room, I'm nude on the JenniCam.
<v Jenni Ringley>嗯，无论我什么时候在房间里裸体，我在珍妮摄像机上就是裸体的。

00:31:59.490 --> 00:32:05.520
<v Ira Glass>But when I think about how often somebody is nude in the course of a day, it's really not very long.
<v Ira Glass>但当我想到一个人一天里裸体的时间有多长时，真的不是很久。

00:32:05.520 --> 00:32:19.140
<v Jenni Ringley>Well, maybe I'm not an average person. But I figure when I'm alone, who really cares? I sleep naked, and I get changed. And when I get out of the shower, I'm all wet. There's no hurry to put on clothes.
<v Jenni Ringley>嗯，也许我不是一个普通人。但我认为当我一个人时，谁真正在乎呢？我裸睡，我换衣服。当我洗完澡出来时，我全身都湿了。没急着穿衣服。

00:32:19.140 --> 00:32:23.830
<v Ira Glass>Explain what the thrill is about being naked in front of a computer camera.
<v Ira Glass>解释一下在电脑摄像头前裸体的快感是什么。

00:32:23.830 --> 00:32:34.710
<v Jenni Ringley>Actually, with the camera there, I don't think about it much. So whenever I'm normally naked in my room, that's when I would be on the camera. It really doesn't affect me in much of a big way, I would say.
<v Jenni Ringley>其实，摄像头在那，我没怎么想它。所以无论我什么时候在房间里正常裸体，那都是我会出现在摄像头上的时候。我想说，它真的没有以大的方式影响我。

00:32:34.710 --> 00:32:37.160
<v Ira Glass>But wait, you're saying it means nothing to you?
<v Ira Glass>但等等，你是说它对你没有任何意义？

00:32:37.160 --> 00:32:58.360
<v Jenni Ringley>I think the camera would be a lot less interesting if I paid that much attention to it. It would be more of a staged show, and you can go see a staged show anywhere. I think the whole appeal of the camera is that it is whatever is normally going on in my room. And with it having been up for a year and a half now, I'm pretty accustomed to it being there. It really doesn't affect me much, I would say.
<v Jenni Ringley>我想如果我那么注意它，摄像头会少有趣得多。它会更像一场精心策划的表演，你可以在任何地方看到一场策划的表演。我想摄像头的全部吸引力在于它就是我房间里正常发生的事情。随着它已经开启了一年半，我已经很习惯它在那里了。我想说，它真的没怎么影响我。

00:32:58.360 --> 00:33:03.630
<v Ira Glass>Have there been any moments over the last two years where you were sort of sorry that the camera was in the dorm room?
<v Ira Glass>这两年里有什么时刻你有点后悔摄像头在宿舍里吗？

00:33:03.630 --> 00:33:27.690
<v Jenni Ringley>Actually, it goes sort of the opposite way. Whenever I go home for breaks, for spring break or something like that, I'm always sad to be away from the camera. It's really a different feeling, whenever I'm in the room and the camera is broken, or for some reason, my room feels totally different. It's like I'm completely alone. So I usually prefer the camera be there, and I'm sad when it's off as opposed to wishing it weren't there.
<v Jenni Ringley>其实，它是反过来的。无论什么时候我回家度假，为了春假或类似的，我总是很伤心远离摄像头。无论我什么时候在房间里，摄像头坏了，或者由于某种原因，我的房间感觉完全不同，感觉真的完全不同。就像我完全一个人。所以我通常更喜欢摄像头在那里，当它关闭时我很伤心，而不是希望它不在那里。

00:33:27.690 --> 00:33:30.060
<v Ira Glass>Because you don't feel alone when it's on.
<v Ira Glass>因为当它开启时你不觉得孤单。

00:33:30.060 --> 00:33:53.150
<v Jenni Ringley>Right. Even though there's nobody actually there with me, even though I'm still alone, even if there's nobody watching the camera from the other end, it's just comforting to know that there is somebody metaphorically out there.
<v Jenni Ringley>对。即使实际上没有人和我在一起，即使我仍然是一个人，即使另一端没有人看着摄像头，知道在隐喻的层面上外面有人在，这很安慰人。

00:33:53.150 --> 00:33:57.460
<v Ira Glass>In your view, why are so many people checking out the site each day?
<v Ira Glass>在你看来，为什么每天有那么多人检查这个网站？

00:33:57.460 --> 00:34:30.910
<v Jenni Ringley>A lot of the people, it's totally hoping to find me getting out of the shower, getting dressed for bed, things like that. It's hoping to find the nudity. But I get lots of email from people who say that it's just nice when they're alone in their office to know that there's somebody else out there, somebody else that is doing nothing more interesting than what they're doing at the same time. It's like having a little virtual friend.
<v Jenni Ringley>很多这些人，完全是希望发现我洗完澡出来，穿睡衣，像那样的事情。它是希望发现裸露。但我收到很多来自人们的电子邮件，说当他们独自在办公室时，知道外面还有一个人，有人做的事情不比他们同时在做的事情有趣，这很好。这就像有一个小小的虚拟朋友。

00:34:30.910 --> 00:34:39.880
<v Ira Glass>Now, at some point in the two years, you've probably had somebody over in the dorm room to mess around.
<v Ira Glass>现在，在这两年里的某个时刻，你可能有客人到宿舍里搞乱。

00:34:39.880 --> 00:34:41.240
<v Jenni Ringley>Sure.
<v Jenni Ringley>当然。

00:34:41.240 --> 00:34:42.020
<v Ira Glass>And?
<v Ira Glass>然后呢？

00:34:42.020 --> 00:35:08.680
<v Jenni Ringley>At that point, it goes on the other person's comfortability. I have no problem doing that. And that's the whole point of the camera is that it's whatever I'm doing. When I went into this, I understood that, in order to make it really work, it would have to be no matter what I was doing. But I can't really enforce that on people who are visiting me. So if the other person is uncomfortable, then the camera is turned off, or it points to a different part of the room.
<v Jenni Ringley>在这一点上，它取决于另一个人的舒适度。我不介意做那个。摄像头的全部意义在于它是我正在做的任何事情。当我进入这个时，我明白，为了让它真正工作，它必须是无论我正在做什么。但我不能真正强迫在访问我的人身上。所以如果另一个人不舒服，那么摄像头被关闭，或者它指向房间的不同部分。

00:35:08.680 --> 00:35:10.970
<v Ira Glass>And generally, has the other person been uncomfortable?
<v Ira Glass>总的来说，另一个人不舒服吗？

00:35:10.970 --> 00:35:14.290
<v Jenni Ringley>Yes. Yes.
<v Jenni Ringley>是的。是的。

00:35:14.290 --> 00:35:20.770
<v Ira Glass>Was there ever a time that you actually had somebody over where you actually kept the camera on the two of you?
<v Ira Glass>有没有什么时候你真正有客人在，你实际上把摄像头保持在你们两个身上？

00:35:20.770 --> 00:35:34.830
<v Jenni Ringley>Yes, in fact. And the funny thing is that it never actually was broadcast because the number of people suddenly reloading on the server ended up crashing the computer that posted the JenniCam at the time.
<v Jenni Ringley>是的，其实。有趣的事情是它从来没有真正播出，因为服务器上突然重载的人数最终搞垮了当时发布珍妮摄像头的电脑。

00:35:34.830 --> 00:35:35.780
<v Ira Glass>Wow.
<v Ira Glass>哇。

00:35:35.780 --> 00:35:43.770
<v Jenni Ringley>So even though we were there and the camera was on, the server was crashed by the number of people wanting to see.
<v Jenni Ringley>所以即使我们在那里，摄像头开着，服务器也被想要观看的人数搞垮了。

00:35:43.770 --> 00:35:47.280
<v Ira Glass>What's your impression of who these people are?
<v Ira Glass>你对这些人是谁有什么印象？

00:35:47.280 --> 00:36:00.530
<v Jenni Ringley>Oh, I don't know. Mostly men, it's almost exclusively men. I get about 700 emails a day. And of that number, maybe 10 are from women.
<v Jenni Ringley>哦，我不知道。大部分是男人，几乎全是男人。我每天收到大约700封电子邮件。在这个数字中，也许10封来自女性。

00:36:00.530 --> 00:36:03.150
<v Ira Glass>You get 700 emails a day?
<v Ira Glass>你每天收到700封电子邮件？

00:36:03.150 --> 00:36:04.600
<v Jenni Ringley>Right.
<v Jenni Ringley>对。

00:36:04.600 --> 00:36:06.360
<v Ira Glass>What are people saying to you?
<v Ira Glass>人们在对你说什么？

00:36:06.360 --> 00:36:42.700
<v Jenni Ringley>Well, a lot of those are entries to a contest on my web page called "Name that Curve" where once a week I put up a new picture of some close-up shot of a part of my body and people guess what it is. So I would say 300 or so of that number are "Name that Curve" entries. The rest of them are people saying either I saw your web page, I like your page, or can I call you, or can you send me private, special pictures? It really, really, ranges.
<v Jenni Ringley>嗯，其中很多是参加我网页上一个叫“命名曲线”的竞赛的条目，每周我都会放上一张我身体某部分的特写照片，人们猜测那是什么。所以我认为这个数字中的300左右是“命名曲线”条目。其余的是人们说我看过你的网页，我喜欢你的页面，或者我可以给你打电话吗，或者你能寄给我私人的、特别的照片吗？它真的，真的，范围很广。

00:36:42.700 --> 00:37:09.330
<v Ira Glass>Jenny says she spends five to six hours a day answering email. And when I talked to her, on the one hand, there seemed to be something completely innocent in what she was doing, putting herself out there and not really caring who sees. And if you press her about her on exhibitionism, she'll tell you over and over, oh, no, no, it's not about exhibitionism. It's an experiment at letting people view a person's entire life without editing.
<v Ira Glass>珍妮说她每天花五到六个小时回答电子邮件。当我谈到她时，一方面，她在做的事情里似乎有一些完全无辜的东西，把自己放出来，并不真正关心谁看。如果你因为她的暴露狂而逼她，她会一遍又一遍地告诉你，哦，不，不，这不是关于暴露狂。这是一个实验，让人们在不编辑的情况下查看一个人的整个生活。

00:37:09.330 --> 00:37:20.090
<v Ira Glass>Though, one thing that she's gotten on the internet that she could never have gotten so easily any other way is she's famous within a small circle. It's a small, particular kind of thing.
<v Ira Glass>虽然，她在互联网上得到的一件她本来无法如此轻易在其他地方得到的事情是，她在一个小圈子里很有名。这是一件小而特别的事情。

00:37:20.090 --> 00:37:29.130
<v Jenni Ringley>I do get a fair number, at this point, of requests for autographed pictures and people wanting to buy my hair and my clothing, things like that.
<v Jenni Ringley>在这一点上，我确实收到了相当多要求签名照片的请求，人们想要买我的头发和我的衣服，类似的东西。

00:37:29.130 --> 00:37:29.510
<v Ira Glass>What?
<v Ira Glass>什么？

00:37:29.510 --> 00:37:45.730
<v Jenni Ringley>It's pretty scary. Well, one time, I was caught on camera actually trimming my bangs, because if you only have to do that, it's cheaper. And all of a sudden, I got 40 emails from people saying, are you going to do anything with that hair? Can I buy it from you?
<v Jenni Ringley>它很吓人。嗯，有一次，我在摄像头上被抓到其实在修剪我的刘海，因为如果你只需要做那个，它更便宜。突然间，我收到了40封来自人们的电子邮件说，你打算用那头发做什么吗？我可以从你那里买吗？

00:37:45.730 --> 00:37:47.340
<v Ira Glass>And what did you make of that?
<v Ira Glass>你对那个做了什么？

00:37:47.340 --> 00:37:56.810
<v Jenni Ringley>Well, it's kind of scary. I mean, I do meet people from time to time. Somebody will say, I'm passing through the area, do you think I could meet you?
<v Jenni Ringley>嗯，有点吓人。我的意思是我确实不时见人。有人会说，我经过这个地区，你认为我可以见你吗？

00:37:56.810 --> 00:37:58.730
<v Ira Glass>And you say?
<v Ira Glass>你说了什么？

00:37:58.730 --> 00:38:13.020
<v Jenni Ringley>I actually have a pretty good knack for getting a good feeling about people right off the bat. So sometimes I say no, sometimes I say yes. I've had dinner with probably a dozen people from the JenniCam.
<v Jenni Ringley>我其实对第一时间对人有一种很好的感觉。所以有时我说不，有时我说是的。我和大概十二个从珍妮摄像头来的人吃过晚饭。

00:38:13.020 --> 00:38:15.470
<v Ira Glass>And has that been nice?
<v Ira Glass>那很好吗？

00:38:15.470 --> 00:38:22.110
<v Jenni Ringley>I had one person who had a hard time taking no for an answer, even after I made it abundantly clear I wasn't interested.
<v Jenni Ringley>我有一个人很难接受不作为答案，即使在我明确表示我不感兴趣之后。

00:38:22.110 --> 00:38:23.650
<v Ira Glass>Really? That's kind of creepy.
<v Ira Glass>真的吗？那有点令人毛骨悚然。

00:38:23.650 --> 00:38:40.450
<v Jenni Ringley>It is, kind of. I've had a fair number of improper passes at the end of the evening. But it stays at a pass.
<v Jenni Ringley>它有点，是的。在晚上结束时，我收到相当多次不适当的搭讪。但它停留在搭讪。

00:38:40.450 --> 00:38:53.180
<v Ira Glass>If there were a cable channel that would just have a camera on in your room, with no sound, 24 hours a day, do you think you'd get a half million viewers?
<v Ira Glass>如果有一个有线频道会在你的房间里一直开着摄像头，没有声音，24小时一天，你认为你会得到50万观众吗？

00:38:53.180 --> 00:39:18.790
<v Jenni Ringley>I don't think so. I don't think I would, because if you have the TV, you have other things you can watch. I think it would still be popular, but I think at that point, it would be a lot less interesting. Because people can do this from their offices, at work, if they have internet usage that's not monitored. From work, you can just put it on and leave it on in the background while you're doing whatever else on your computer.
<v Jenni Ringley>我不这么认为。我不认为我会，因为如果你有电视，你有其他可以看的东西。我认为它仍然会很受欢迎，但我认为在这一点上，它会少有趣得多。因为人们可以在他们的办公室做这个，在工作时，如果他们有不受监控的互联网使用。从工作处，你可以只是把它打开并让它在后台开着，同时你在电脑上做其他任何事情。

00:39:18.790 --> 00:39:34.080
<v Ira Glass>Jenni Ringley has just graduated from college. She's moved to another city, where she has gotten a job designing web pages for a big national magazine. [APPLAUSE]
<v Ira Glass>珍妮·林利刚刚大学毕业。她搬到了另一个城市，在那里她得到了一份为一家大型国家杂志设计网页的工作。（掌声）

00:39:34.080 --> 00:39:55.470
<v Ira Glass>OK. It's me again in the present. Jenni Ringley and the JenniCam were internet celebrities for seven years, written about all the time. There were parody websites, JenniCam-ing people's pets and stuff like that. She was invited on the Letterman show. She was included in a 2001 exhibit done by the Museum of Modern Art. The word JenniCam was reportedly, for a while, one of the most popular search terms on Slashdot.
<v Ira Glass>好的。又是现在的我。珍妮·林利和珍妮摄像头做了七年的互联网名流，一直被写到。有恶搞网站，珍妮摄像头搞人们的宠物之类的东西。她被邀请上莱特曼秀。她被包括在现代艺术博物馆2001年的展览中。据报道，珍妮摄像头这个词有一段时间是Slashdot上最流行的搜索词之一。

00:39:55.470 --> 00:40:15.940
<v Ira Glass>Until finally, at the end of 2003, Jenni Ringley shut down the site. According to Wikipedia, fans started to turn on her in part because they were annoyed that she just would loaf around the apartment. There was a feeling of, like, get a job. By the end, people had to pay to log on to the site, and she actually was making decent money from it, or seemed to. And this annoyed fans as well.
<v Ira Glass>直到最后，在2003年底，珍妮·林利关闭了网站。据维基百科，粉丝开始背离她，部分是因为他们很恼火她只是在公寓里游手好闲。有一种想法，比如，找份工作。到最后，人们必须付费登录网站，她实际上从中赚了不错的钱，或者看起来是这样。这也惹恼了粉丝。

00:40:15.940 --> 00:40:32.940
<v Ira Glass>To the very end, she continued to describe the site as a kind of art project. "I keep JenniCam alive not because I want or need to be watched, but because I simply don't mind being watched," she wrote. "What you see is my life, exactly as it would be, whether or not there were cameras watching, as a chronicle, a long-term experiment."
<v Ira Glass>直到最后，她继续将该网站描述为一种艺术项目。“我保持珍妮摄像头活跃不是因为我想要或需要被观看，而是因为我根本不介意被观看，”她写道。“你看到的是我的生活，正如它将会是的那样，无论是否有摄像头在观看，作为一种编年史，一个长期的实验。”

00:40:32.940 --> 00:40:42.220
<v Ira Glass>Well, now, this example of people's lives changed by the internet, Eileen and Fred Kiefer live outside Columbus, Ohio. They are septuagenarians with seven kids and 13 grandkids.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，现在，这个互联网改变人们生活的例子，艾琳和弗雷德·基弗住在俄亥俄州哥伦布市外。他们是七个孩子和13个孙辈的七旬老人。

00:40:42.220 --> 00:40:53.410
<v Eileen Kiefer>OK, well, when we first got our email going on our computer, I sent a message to our son in Milwaukee whose address was K-I-E-F-F.
<v Eileen Kiefer>好的，当我们第一次在电脑上搞定电子邮件时，我给我儿子发了一条信息，他在密尔沃基，他的地址是K-I-E-F-F。

00:40:53.410 --> 00:40:54.090
<v Ira Glass>K-I-E-F-F?
<v Ira Glass>K-I-E-F-F？

00:40:54.090 --> 00:41:04.160
<v Eileen Kiefer>Yeah. It was my first time to use it, and I left one of the F's off.
<v Eileen Kiefer>对。这是我第一次使用它，我漏掉了一个F。

00:41:04.160 --> 00:41:10.840
<v Ira Glass>Her email was a chatty email about everything going on in the family, signed "love, Mom."
<v Ira Glass>她的电子邮件是一封关于家庭中发生的一切的聊天邮件，签名为“爱你的，妈妈”。

00:41:10.840 --> 00:41:44.810
<v Eileen Kiefer>So I got back a letter from someone whose address was K-I-E-F. And he said, I enjoyed this letter, but I don't think you meant it for me. And so then I wrote back and said, thanks and explained how I'd made the mistake. And he wrote back. And we've been writing now for, it'll be two years this month.
<v Eileen Kiefer>所以我收到了一个人的回信，他的地址是K-I-E-F。他说，我喜欢这封信，但我不认为你是写给我的。所以我就回信说，谢谢并解释了我怎么犯的错误。他又回信了。我们现在已经写了，这个月就是两年了。

00:41:44.810 --> 00:42:10.150
<v Ira Glass>The man, the couple actually, who got her email were Kiefer and Galen Mitchell in Portland, Oregon. He works at Tandy and does a radio show out there. And when he got Mrs. Kiefer's email in his account, the thing that actually got to him was that she signed it "love, Mom." His own mother had just died two months before. His father had been dead for years. Mrs. Kiefer said she never intended or wanted to have a long-term email friendship.
<v Ira Glass>收到她邮件的男人，这对夫妇，是俄勒冈州波特兰市的基弗和盖伦·米切尔。他在Tandy工作，并在那里做电台节目。当他在他的账户收到基弗夫人的电子邮件时，实际上击中他的是她签名为“爱你的，妈妈”。他自己的母亲刚在两个月前去世。他的父亲几年前就去世了。基弗夫人说她从来没有打算或想要有一个长期的电子邮件友谊。

00:42:10.150 --> 00:42:32.660
<v Eileen Kiefer>It seems like we had nothing in common going in. The age difference, and they had no children, and they're Mormons and we're Catholics. So it seemed like there wasn't a whole lot that we had to talk about. But we never have any trouble.
<v Eileen Kiefer>看起来我们在进入时没有任何共同点。年龄差距，他们没有孩子，他们是摩门教徒，我们是天主教徒。所以看起来我们没什么可谈的。但我们从来没有困难。

00:42:32.660 --> 00:42:50.030
<v Eileen Kiefer>At first, we were writing every day. We were both so excited about this. And now, we're usually in touch at least once a week. And we've visited, and he's in Oregon. We're in Ohio. We visited in Portland last year and spent a couple days with him and had a wonderful time.
<v Eileen Kiefer>起初，我们每天都在写。我们都对这个很兴奋。现在，我们通常至少每周联系一次。而且我们访问过，他在俄勒冈州。我们在俄亥俄州。我们去年在波特兰访问过，和他一起度过了几天，度过了一段美好的时光。

00:42:50.030 --> 00:43:21.870
<v Eileen Kiefer>And they're like 30 years younger than we are. So we've become mom and dad, and we've adopted them. We call them our legally adopted children. And in December, when I had a serious operation, he was on the phone every night, just like the rest of our kids. So he's really become one of our kids.
<v Eileen Kiefer>他们比我们年轻30岁左右。所以我们成为了妈妈和爸爸，我们收养了他们。我们称他们为我们法律上收养的孩子。而在12月，当我做了严重的手术时，他每晚都在电话上，就像我们其余的孩子一样。所以他真的成为了我们孩子之一。

00:43:21.870 --> 00:43:29.240
<v Ira Glass>So what in the world was it in those first emails they sent two years ago? What could people possibly say to create a bond like that?
<v Ira Glass>那么，在那两年前他们发送的第一批电子邮件里到底是什么？人们可能说什么来创造那样的纽带？

00:43:29.240 --> 00:43:44.750
<v Eileen Kiefer>Oh, I don't remember. I just remember indicating that I was 70. So there was-- I didn't want anybody to think that we were going to have this big romance or anything.
<v Eileen Kiefer>哦，我不记得了。我只记得我指出我是70岁。所以有——我不想让任何人认为我们将要有一场大的浪漫之类的。

00:43:44.750 --> 00:43:45.120
<v Ira Glass>And then--
<v Ira Glass>然后——

00:43:45.120 --> 00:44:01.530
<v Eileen Kiefer>Because you read these stories about people doing weird things with people on the internet. We never had that problem.
<v Eileen Kiefer>因为你读过这些关于人们在互联网上与人做奇怪事情的故事。我们从来没有那个问题。

00:44:01.530 --> 00:44:13.930
<v Eileen Kiefer>We have seven children. And all of them have email except one. And the ones out of town, we hear from a couple times a week. One of our daughters, we hear from almost daily.
<v Eileen Kiefer>我们有七个孩子。他们除了一个以外都有电子邮件。住在城外的那些，我们每周听到几次。我们的一个女儿，我们几乎每天都听到。

00:44:13.930 --> 00:44:16.360
<v Ira Glass>Now, did you have as much contact with them before email?
<v Ira Glass>现在，在电子邮件之前，你和他们有那么多的联系吗？

00:44:16.360 --> 00:44:35.330
<v Eileen Kiefer>Oh, no. No. We would talk on the phone maybe once a month. And it was always, how are you? And you never really were part of their lives that way. And two of our children live in Boston. And we just weren't-- well, we were always close. We weren't communicating very often.
<v Eileen Kiefer>哦，不。不。我们也许一个月打一次电话。总是，你怎么样？你从来没有真正那样成为他们生活的一部分。我们的两个孩子住在波士顿。我们只是没有——嗯，我们一直很亲近。我们没有经常交流。

00:44:35.330 --> 00:44:37.480
<v Ira Glass>So email has really brought you all closer together.
<v Ira Glass>所以电子邮件真的让你们所有人更亲近了。

00:44:37.480 --> 00:45:09.110
<v Eileen Kiefer>Oh, it's wonderful. I keep trying to talk everybody I know into it, if their children have access to a computer. It's changed our life a lot. It's just changed our lives. I wouldn't think of not checking the computer first thing in the morning to see who's writing.
<v Eileen Kiefer>哦，太棒了。我一直尝试说服所有我认识的人使用它，如果他们的孩子有电脑使用权的话。它极大地改变了我们的生活。它只是改变了我们的生活。我不会想到早上第一件事不检查电脑看看是谁在写信。

00:45:09.110 --> 00:45:16.390
<v Ira Glass>Eileen Kiefer in Dublin, Ohio.
<v Ira Glass>艾琳·基弗在俄亥俄州都柏林。

00:45:16.390 --> 00:46:37.750
<v Ira Glass>[APPLAUSE]
<v Ira Glass>（掌声）

00:46:37.750 --> 00:46:58.530
<v Ira Glass>Well, as we looked for stories from the net for this show, especially stories of things happened on the net that might not happen anywhere else, we found Earl Jackson. He's an Associate Professor of Comparative Literature at the University of California at Santa Cruz, and he spends a lot of time on the net. He writes about the net, has seven websites himself. And he tells this story, which begins on America Online.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，当我们寻找关于这个节目的网络故事时，特别是网上发生的可能不会在其他任何地方发生的事情的故事，我们发现了厄尔·杰克逊。他是加州大学圣克鲁兹分校的比较文学副教授，他花很多时间在网上。他写关于网络，自己有七个网站。他讲了这个故事，它始于美国在线。

00:46:58.530 --> 00:47:24.560
<v Earl Jackson>I was on AOL and other services in San Francisco a lot. And there are these chat rooms where gay men can meet and talk. And in San Francisco, it's fairly wild. There's a 24-hour bulletin board that I was observing for this study I was doing, where you could almost be guaranteed that if you lived in San Francisco and you had a modem, you could have sex any time of the day or night. This was a very goal-directed bulletin board.
<v Earl Jackson>我在旧金山有很多AOL和其他服务。这里有同性恋男人可以见面和交谈的聊天室。在旧金山，它相当狂野。有一个24小时公告板，我为我正在做的这项研究观察过，在那里你几乎可以保证，如果你住在旧金山，并且你有一个调制解调器，你可以一天或晚上的任何时间做爱。这是一个目标明确的公告板。

00:47:24.560 --> 00:47:26.700
<v Ira Glass>Right. People would meet, go have sex.
<v Ira Glass>对。人们见面，去做爱。

00:47:26.700 --> 00:47:50.470
<v Earl Jackson>Right. And you would know exactly what they wanted to do, and you would probably have nude pictures of them before that. What I also thought was really interesting-- and this  was more true of AOL-- that there was a new metaphysics of sexuality. Because people would talk about, cyber-sex or real sex? And it occurred to me after I listened to enough, by real sex they meant phone sex.
<v Earl Jackson>对。而且你会确切地知道他们想做什么，并且你可能在那之前就有他们的裸体照片。我也认为非常有趣的是——这在AOL中更真实——有一种关于性的新形而上学。因为人们会谈论，网络性还是真实性？我在听够了之后意识到，他们所指的真实性是电话性。

00:47:50.470 --> 00:47:51.310
<v Ira Glass>Oh, really?
<v Ira Glass>哦，真的吗？

00:47:51.310 --> 00:47:59.740
<v Earl Jackson>Yeah, so it became, instead of a two-tier system, it's a three-tier system. And you have real sex, ultra-real sex, and cyber-sex.
<v Earl Jackson>是啊，所以它变成了，而不是一个双层系统，它是一个三层系统。你有真实性，超真实性，和网络性。

00:47:59.740 --> 00:48:01.230
<v Ira Glass>Wait, ultra-real sex, you said?
<v Ira Glass>等等，超真实性，你说？

00:48:01.230 --> 00:48:05.770
<v Earl Jackson>That would mean what I call a slow-time interface, which means actual physical contact.
<v Earl Jackson>那将意味着我称之为慢时间接口，这意味着实际的物理接触。

00:48:05.770 --> 00:48:07.230
<v Ira Glass>God, do people still do that?
<v Ira Glass>上帝，人们还在做那个吗？

00:48:07.230 --> 00:48:17.010
<v Earl Jackson>Um, yes.
<v Earl Jackson>嗯，是的。

00:48:17.010 --> 00:48:26.200
<v Ira Glass>Earl Jackson says that he meets more and more people who have hooked up what amounts to video cameras on their computers with a technology called CU-SeeMe, they can look at the person they're interacting with over the net.
<v Ira Glass>厄尔·杰克逊说他见到越来越多的人，他们把相当于视频摄像机的东西接在他们的电脑上，使用一种叫CU-SeeMe的技术，他们可以看到他们在网上互动的人。

00:48:26.200 --> 00:48:34.040
<v Earl Jackson>I know people that actually leave physical dates to go home and have sex over the net with the quick cam.
<v Earl Jackson>我知道有人实际上离开物理约会，回家通过快速摄像头在网上做爱。

00:48:34.040 --> 00:48:34.810
<v Ira Glass>Come
<v Ira Glass>来

00:48:34.810 --> 00:48:42.400
<v Earl Jackson>It's true. There's an entire culture that's entirely video transmission sexuality now.
<v Earl Jackson>这是真的。现在有一种完全是视频传输性的文化。

00:48:42.400 --> 00:48:46.040
<v Ira Glass>Talk a little more about that. You actually know somebody who actually left an actual date--
<v Ira Glass>多谈一点那个。你实际上认识某人，他实际上离开了一个实际的约会——

00:48:46.040 --> 00:48:47.040
<v Earl Jackson>Yeah, me, in fact.
<v Earl Jackson>是啊，事实上是我。

00:48:47.040 --> 00:48:47.910
<v Ira Glass>Oh, they left you?
<v Ira Glass>哦，他们离开你了？

00:48:47.910 --> 00:49:00.610
<v Earl Jackson>Yeah, they left me. But he said that that is what he does lately. And I understood that, because a lot of men are afraid of sexual contact because of AIDS. So the perfect thing to eroticize is distance.
<v Earl Jackson>是啊，他们离开我了。但他确实说那是他最近做的。我理解了，因为很多男人因为艾滋病而害怕性接触。所以完美的事情是把距离情色化。

00:49:00.610 --> 00:49:07.570
<v Ira Glass>And so he says to you-- at what point in the evening did he say, I'm going to go home now?
<v Ira Glass>所以他对你说——他在晚上的什么时刻说，我现在要回家了？

00:49:07.570 --> 00:49:16.560
<v Earl Jackson>Pretty soon. He said that he really needed to do the CU-SeeMe stuff.
<v Earl Jackson>很快。他说他确实需要做CU-SeeMe的东西。

00:49:16.560 --> 00:49:42.290
<v Ira Glass>Back in December of 1993, Earl Jackson met a guy over the net in one of these gay chat rooms. The guy's name was Ken, who lived nearby, but he had a boyfriend so the two never met in person. But almost every day for a year, they got online together and created these elaborate fantasies together online, using that kind of software where you can see what the other person types as they type it, and they can see what you type as you type it.
<v Ira Glass>早在1993年12月，厄尔·杰克逊在这些同性恋聊天室之一中通过网络认识了一个家伙。这家伙的名字叫肯，住在附近，但他有一个男朋友，所以两人从来没有亲自见面。但几乎每一天，持续了一年，他们在线聚在一起，通过使用那种可以看到对方打字时打什么，并且他们可以看到你打字时打什么的软件，一起创造了这些复杂的幻想。

00:49:42.290 --> 00:50:02.710
<v Earl Jackson>We would have a fantasy about what if he came to my classroom and then I took him back into my office. But it was really sort of a game. But these narratives became so intense that we would set times of the day, or he would just check in. And he said, do you have time for a story now? And he would start it, and then I would continue it. And we have all of these stories.
<v Earl Jackson>我们有一个幻想，关于如果他来到我的教室会发生什么，然后我把他带回我的办公室。但它真的像是一个游戏。但这些叙事变得如此强烈，以至于我们会设定一天中的时间，或者他只是会签到。他说，你现在有时间讲故事吗？他会开始它，然后我会继续它。我们有所有这些故事。

00:50:02.710 --> 00:50:17.640
<v Earl Jackson>But as we kept doing this, then a little part of his life would come in. And then there would be a story that would take us somewhere else. And then I'd know a little bit more. And soon the sex part was really an excuse to tell the other stories.
<v Earl Jackson>但当我们继续做这个时，然后他生活的一小部分会进来。然后会有一个故事把我们带到其他地方。然后我会知道多一点。很快性部分真的只是讲其他故事的借口。

00:50:17.640 --> 00:50:40.800
<v Earl Jackson>And sometimes he would be telling this story about his childhood in Missouri. And then it would remind him of a sexual fantasy. And then he'd put me in it. And you couldn't plan to do these things. We became sort of like jazz pianists or something. We'd have these riffs together.
<v Earl Jackson>有时他会讲这个关于他在密苏里州的童年的故事。然后它会提醒他一个性幻想。然后他会把我放进去。你不能计划做这些事情。我们变得有点像爵士钢琴家什么的。我们一起有这些即兴片段。

00:50:40.800 --> 00:50:49.300
<v Earl Jackson>Then one day, he was asking me if I had time to talk to him. And I was just leaving, but I could tell that even the way he was typing was different.
<v Earl Jackson>有一天，他问我是否有时间和他说话。我正要离开，但我能看出即使他打字的方式也不同。

00:50:49.300 --> 00:50:52.050
<v Ira Glass>You could tell even the way he was typing was different?
<v Ira Glass>你甚至能看出他打字的方式也不同？

00:50:52.050 --> 00:50:53.100
<v Earl Jackson>Yes, yes.
<v Earl Jackson>是啊，是啊。

00:50:53.100 --> 00:50:54.050
<v Ira Glass>What do you mean?
<v Ira Glass>你是什么意思？

00:50:54.050 --> 00:51:10.520
<v Earl Jackson>People have different habits in their speed, or the way he would respond, or if he didn't put a smiley face after a certain number of words. I just knew there was something really wrong with the way he asked me if I had time.
<v Earl Jackson>人们在他们的速度方面有不同的习惯，或者他会回复的方式，或者如果他在一定数量的词语之后没有放一个笑脸。我只是知道他在问我是否有时间时，某种方式确实错了。

00:51:10.520 --> 00:51:41.360
<v Earl Jackson>And he said, bad news, son. And I think I knew it instantly, what that was. He had tested positive. And he didn't know how to break it to me. And our messages to each other then became a lot more about that and about what happened to him as a child and other things that were fairly tragic and amazing that he was telling anybody, because he was really one of those strong, silent types.
<v Earl Jackson>他说，坏消息，儿子。我想我立即知道那是什么。他检测呈阳性。而他不知道如何把它突破给我。我们给彼此的信息那时变得更多关于那个，关于他小时候发生了什么，以及其他相当悲剧和令人惊奇的东西，他在告诉任何人，因为他确实是那种坚强、沉默的类型之一。

00:51:41.360 --> 00:52:04.270
<v Earl Jackson>Then-- this is when I should have gotten more nervous-- he started saying that he didn't want any of his porn tapes anymore because he associated the porn with him being positive. So he wanted to mail them to me, and he mailed me a box of them. I didn't really want them, but he wanted to do this for me. So there was suddenly a box of porn tapes in my house. And then the following week, there was one too.
<v Earl Jackson>然后——这是当我应该变得更紧张的时候——他开始说他不想要任何他的色情磁带了，因为他把色情与他阳性联系起来。所以他想要邮寄给我，他寄给我一盒。我并不真正想要它们，但他想为我做这个。所以突然间我的房子里有一盒色情磁带。然后接下来的一周，也有一盒。

00:52:04.270 --> 00:52:52.770
<v Earl Jackson>And then just before I got back to San Francisco, I got a message from him, hoping that I was well and hoping I got the tapes. And he said that I'd get another message very soon. Now, he was writing to me almost entirely in capital letters, which scared me. And I didn't know why. But here's the last one. "I have it all boxed up now. I will give it my friend. He will mail it for me like he did before. And it will arrive either Thursday or Friday, via two-day express. I have a letter coming to you to help explain a few things. Thank you for being a friend when I really needed it. Kenneth."
<v Earl Jackson>就在我回到旧金山之前，我收到他的一条信息，希望我好，希望我收到磁带。他说我会很快收到另一条信息。现在，他几乎完全用大写字母给我写信，这吓坏我了。我不知道为什么。但这里是最后一条。“我把它全部打包了。我会给它我的朋友。他会为我邮寄它，就像他之前做的那样。它会周四或周五到达，通过两天快递。我有一封信给你来帮助解释一些事情。谢谢你在我确实需要时作为一个朋友。肯尼斯。”

00:52:52.770 --> 00:53:03.610
<v Earl Jackson>And then three days, nothing. And then there's just one line. "A package should arrive today. 1/19/95. Email me. Cruiser3. Bye. Love, Ken."
<v Earl Jackson>然后三天，什么都没有。然后只有一行。“包裹今天应该到达。1/19/95。电邮我。Cruiser3。再见。爱你的，肯。”

00:53:03.610 --> 00:53:46.220
<v Earl Jackson>And then on the 25th, there was one that looked like it was from Ken. It was his screen name. But then, when I opened it up, it said, "this is a hard note for me to write. Ken left a note to ask me to let you know Ken took his own life on Friday, January 20. He was cremated yesterday, and his ashes will be dropped into the ocean off of San Francisco today. Ken's mental attitude over the last four to six weeks is very hard to describe. He was a basket case to say the least. I will keep his account open for the next day if you have any questions or response, and I will try to answer them for you. Ken's friend, Dave."
<v Earl Jackson>然后在25号，有一条看起来是来自肯的。它是他的屏幕名称。但后来，当我打开它时，它说，“这是我很难写的字条。肯留下了一个字条请求我让你知道肯在周五，1月20日自杀了。他昨天被火化了，他的骨灰今天会被撒进旧金山的海洋里。肯在过去四到六周的精神态度很难描述。至少可以说是崩溃了。如果有什么问题或回应，我会保持他的账户开放一天，我会尝试为你回答。肯的朋友，戴夫。”

00:53:46.220 --> 00:53:50.020
<v Ira Glass>Did he send you a final note in the last package he sent you?
<v Ira Glass>他在他寄给你的最后包裹里寄给你最后一张字条吗？

00:53:50.020 --> 00:54:02.630
<v Earl Jackson>Yeah. It was the only time I ever saw his handwriting. And that said similar things. He said, thank you for being a friend when I needed it. And I would always love you.
<v Earl Jackson>是啊。这是我唯一一次看到他的笔迹。那上面说类似的事情。他说，谢谢你在我需要时作为一个朋友。我永远会爱你。

00:54:02.630 --> 00:54:09.350
<v Ira Glass>It must have been so strange to see a physical manifestation of him after the email.
<v Ira Glass>在电子邮件之后看到他的物理表现一定很奇怪。

00:54:09.350 --> 00:54:35.900
<v Earl Jackson>It was. And what was really odd was that, when he first sent the first box of tapes, he told me which tape had a scene where the two people are the ones that he imagined us to be. Now, that doesn't mean that he looked like either one of them, but he knew the fantasies that would resonate with us. So even at that point, our fantasies were constantly mediated by some other technology, which is probably hard for you to imagine.
<v Earl Jackson>它是。而且真正奇怪的是，当他第一次寄来第一盒磁带时，他告诉我哪一盘磁带有一个场景，其中两个人是他想象我们成为的人。现在，那并不意味着他看起来像他们中的任何一个，但他知道会引起我们共鸣的幻想。所以即使在那个时候，我们的幻想也被其他技术不断调解，这可能是你们很难想象的。

00:54:35.900 --> 00:54:54.410
<v Earl Jackson>But none of this was cold. There was something so tender about this that I was very moved by this experience.
<v Earl Jackson>但这些都不是冷的。关于这个，有一些如此温柔的东西，以至于我被这种经历非常感动。

00:54:54.410 --> 00:54:57.400
<v Ira Glass>After he died, did you go through a period of mourning for him?
<v Ira Glass>在他死后，你是否经历过一段为他哀悼的时期？

00:54:57.400 --> 00:54:59.770
<v Earl Jackson>Yes, I did. Yes.
<v Earl Jackson>是的，我有。是的。

00:54:59.770 --> 00:55:04.030
<v Ira Glass>What a strange thing to be mourning somebody who you never actually saw.
<v Ira Glass>哀悼一个你从来没有真正见到的人，真是一件奇怪的事情。

00:55:04.030 --> 00:55:25.670
<v Earl Jackson>Yeah. Although, it's real. You know, when people say, oh, the computers are making us all isolated and it's such a cold world, I've had emotional experiences and long-term friendships that would have never possible otherwise.
<v Earl Jackson>是啊。虽然，它是真实的。你知道，当人们说，哦，电脑正在让我们所有人孤立，那是一个如此冷酷的世界，我确实有过情感体验和长期的友谊，这些本来是不可能的。

00:55:25.670 --> 00:55:33.900
<v Ira Glass>It's funny because it's almost like the whole thing, it could be a con, an elaborate con.
<v Ira Glass>这很有趣，因为它几乎就像整个事情，它可能是一个骗局，一个复杂的骗局。

00:55:33.900 --> 00:55:54.870
<v Earl Jackson>He would be such a creep that-- I thought of that, actually. Because it seemed almost like a melodrama from a long time ago. It was really tawdry. And if I wrote a short story, I wouldn't end it this way because it was too hokey.
<v Earl Jackson>他会是如此的令人毛骨悚然，以至于——我确实想到了那个，事实上。因为它看起来几乎像很久以前的通俗剧。它真的很俗气。如果我写短篇小说，我不会以这种方式结束它，因为它太陈腐了。

00:55:54.870 --> 00:56:10.420
<v Ira Glass>After he died, you know how after somebody you're close to is gone, how when you go back to the places where you used to go with them, you'll think of them inevitably and miss them. After he was gone, when you would get back on your computer, would you sense his loss?
<v Ira Glass>在他死后，你知道在和你亲近的人离开后，当回到你曾经和他们一起去的地方时，你会不可避免地想到他们并想念他们。在他走后，当你回到你的电脑时，你会感到他的损失吗？

00:56:10.420 --> 00:56:29.200
<v Earl Jackson>Yes. He really felt gone. He really felt gone. When I saw somebody else using his screen name, I mean, to get a stranger with his screen name, that was really, really chilling.
<v Earl Jackson>是的。他确实感到走了。他确实感到走了。当我看到别人使用他的屏幕名称时，我的意思，得到一个用他屏幕名称的陌生人，那真的，真的很冷。

00:56:29.200 --> 00:56:49.320
<v Ira Glass>Earl Jackson in Santa Cruz.
<v Ira Glass>厄尔·杰克逊在圣克鲁兹。

00:56:49.320 --> 00:57:06.750
